{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    output_list = list(l) # Create a shallow copy of the input list.\n\n    divisible_by_three_elements = []\n    divisible_by_three_indices = []\n\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            divisible_by_three_elements.append(l[i])\n            divisible_by_three_indices.append(i)\n\n    divisible_by_three_elements.sort() # Sort the collected elements.\n\n    for i, original_index in enumerate(divisible_by_three_indices):\n        output_list[original_index] = divisible_by_three_elements[i]\n\n    return output_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\n\ndef _validate_find_zero_inputs(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates input for find_zero: checks even length and non-zero highest coefficient.\n    Raises ValueError if validation fails according to NFRs.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not xs:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list xs cannot be empty.\")\n    if len(xs) % 2 != 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list xs must have an even number of coefficients.\")\n    # The highest degree coefficient is xs[len(xs)-1]\n    if abs(xs[-1]) < 1e-9:\n        raise ValueError(\"The highest degree coefficient must be non-zero.\")\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_find_zero_inputs(xs)\n\n    tolerance = 1e-6\n    max_bisection_iterations = 100\n\n    # Initialize a small interval around zero and check for a root.\n    a, b = -1.0, 1.0\n    f_a = poly(xs, a)\n    f_b = poly(xs, b)\n\n    # Check if 0 is a root (i.e., xs[0] is zero)\n    if abs(xs[0]) < tolerance:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # Expand the search interval [a, b] until a sign change is found\n    # (guaranteed for odd-degree polynomials) or a boundary is a root.\n    expand_factor = 2.0\n    max_expand_iterations = 100 \n    for _ in range(max_expand_iterations):\n        if f_a * f_b < 0:  # A root is bracketed\n            break\n        \n        # Expand interval symmetrically\n        a *= expand_factor\n        b *= expand_factor\n        \n        f_a = poly(xs, a)\n        f_b = poly(xs, b)\n\n        # Check for roots at the expanded boundaries\n        if abs(f_a) < tolerance:\n            return a\n        if abs(f_b) < tolerance:\n            return b\n            \n        # Prevent excessive expansion for very large roots or numerical issues.\n        if abs(a) > 1e12 or abs(b) > 1e12:\n            break # Exit if bounds become too large without bracketing\n\n    # Bisection method to find the root within the bracketed interval [a, b].\n    for _ in range(max_bisection_iterations):\n        mid = (a + b) / 2\n        f_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        if abs(f_mid) < tolerance:\n            return mid\n\n        if poly(xs, a) * f_mid < 0:  # Root is in the lower half [a, mid]\n            b = mid\n        else:  # Root is in the upper half [mid, b]\n            a = mid\n\n    # If max_bisection_iterations reached, return the midpoint of the final interval\n    return (a + b) / 2"}
